  <div class="story">
    <div><h3>The problem</h3><p>Most AI consultancies sell slides. Noria sells working software, so its site had to behave like evidence: fast, precise, and visibly engineered.</p><p>It also had to turn a visitor into a scheduled conversation in two languages and across time zones, without my running a database or a third-party booking service for a three-person studio.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What I built</h3><p>I designed and wrote the whole site: seven sections that alternate between a dark cyan theme and a cream orange one, a work page, an about page with game-style team cards, and every canvas effect by hand.</p><p>The booking flow is mine end to end: a slot generator, a three-step form that survives a language switch, two emails, and a calendar invite built line by line.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What made it hard</h3><p>Time. A slot is "9:00 in Austin", which is a different instant in winter than in summer, and the visitor may be in Quito, where clocks never change. JavaScript has no built-in way to turn a wall-clock time in a named zone into an instant, so I derived it from the browser's own formatter.</p><p>The second problem was motion budget: four canvas systems and a pinned horizontal scroll that must stay smooth on a phone.</p></div>
  </div>

